{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1319,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pretty much everything in the universe is\nmade out of matter - the earth, air, you and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6149,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "me, stars, interstellar dust - all matter.\nBy which we mean that these things are made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10740,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of electrons and quarks - and very occasionally\nother rarer matter particles like muons, tauons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16650,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and neutrinos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17890,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of these particles are, at their fundamental\nlevel, excitations in everywhere-permeating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22369,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum fields. But, as the famous quote goes,\n“for every particle, there is an equal and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27259,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposite antiparticle - an opposite excitation\nin the everywhere permeating quantum field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31960,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that has all of the exact same properties\nas that particle - except opposite charge.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36620,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since these antiparticles are opposite\nexcitations of the quantum field, when a particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40989,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and antiparticle meet, they annihilate and\ndestroy each other!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44719,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is pretty much exactly like how the\nequation x^2=4 has two solutions: 2 and -2,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50760,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the same value but opposite sign. And\nwhen they meet, they annihilate!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55510,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every fundamental particle has an antiparticle:\nthere are antiquarks, antineutrinos, antimuons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60350,
    "dDurationMs": 5559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antitauons, and of course antielectrons - though\nwe call them positrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65909,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since antimatter particles are essentially\nidentical to regular matter other than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69680,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposite charge thing, they can combine together\nin essentially identical ways to form antiprotons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74980,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-atoms, anti-molecules, and, in principle,\nanything from anti-ants to anti-matterhorns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80000,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can also make the really cool positronium atom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82800,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it’s like hydrogen, except instead of an electron orbiting a proton,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86000,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s an electron orbiting a POSITRON. Until\nthey annihilate each other in under a nanosecond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91300,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because every particle of antimatter annihilates\nwith regular matter upon meeting, it’s really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95590,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard to make anything big out of antimatter\n- at this point we’re still only able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100090,
    "dDurationMs": 2689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make and contain a few hundred antihydrogen atoms at one time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103540,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when they annihilate, the energy of particle\nand antiparticle has to go somewhere, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107840,
    "dDurationMs": 4989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is why matter/antimatter annihilations have\nbeen proposed as bombs. But naturally-occuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112829,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter is hard to come by. So, unlike\na uranium fission bomb, which allow us to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117560,
    "dDurationMs": 4159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "release the bottled energy of the supernovas\nthat forged the uranium in the first place,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121719,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’d have to put all the energy into an\nantimatter bomb yourself by making antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127049,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which you do by agitating empty space into\npairs of matter and antimatter excitations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kind of like hitting zero with a hammer to\nget out 2 and minus 2, except instead of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136680,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hammer, you use a particle accelerator or\nhigh-energy photons of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140540,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Photons, incidentally, have zero charge and\nso are their OWN antiparticles, in the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145310,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way that zero is equal to negative zero!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147590,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, mathematics has always been closely\ntied to antimatter: the mathematics of relativisitic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152770,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics predicted the existence\nof antimatter before any had ever been discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157860,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that there’s so little antimatter\naround in the universe TO discover is both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162020,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an obvious thing (because if it were around,\nit would have destroyed us), a good thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165530,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(because it can’t destroy us), and a puzzling\nthing - if matter and antimatter are basically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identical “mirror” images of one another,\nwhy did the big bang produce so much more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174360,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter than antimatter?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
